'b1-921f91be', 'content': '## Discovery and archaeological context\n\nModern 
discussions of the Ring of Nestor distinguish it clearly from the four gold 
seal rings discovered in 2015 in the so‑called Griffin Warrior tomb near the 
Palace of Nestor at Pylos, even though both sets of material are frequently 
mentioned together in studies of Mycenaean elite culture.[3] The Ring of 
Nestor, catalogued as CMS I, 179, is reported in a detailed study by Greek 
archaeologist Apostolos Arvanitopoulos to have been unearthed in December 1915 
at the Mycenaean site of Tiryns, rather than at Pylos.[2] In that study the 
ring is described in terms of its dimensions, weight and context within the 
Tiryns finds, and is treated as part of a corpus of Mycenaean gold signet rings
rather than as an object from a closed burial comparable to the Griffin Warrior
assemblage.[2]\n\nBy contrast, the Griffin Warrior tomb excavated near the 
Palace of Nestor contained a richly furnished burial with weapons, jewellery 
and seal stones, including four gold rings with complex engraved scenes, which 
have been described by Shari Stocker and Jack L. Davis of the University of 
Cincinnati as evidence for close engagement between mainland elites and Minoan 
religious imagery.[3] Stocker has emphasized the high level of craftsmanship 
involved in producing the rings without modern tools, while Davis has argued 
that the selection of grave goods in the tomb reflects a deliberate choice of 
objects that conveyed religious messages intelligible to their mainland owner 
rather than an arbitrary collection of loot.[3] In later commentary, Davis has 
also stated that the excavators remain uncertain whether the Griffin Warrior 
was buried by Minoans or by Mycenaeans who had adopted elements of Minoan 
culture, underlining that questions of cultural attribution must be handled 
cautiously.[3]', 'evidence_refs': ['e13', 'e14', 'e15', 'e16', 'e17', 'e18', 
