commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token': 
'b3-4add62c4', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is a substantial gold signet ring
whose physical characteristics have attracted attention in studies of Mycenaean
seal rings. In modern scholarship the ring is catalogued as CMS I, 179 and is 
described as weighing about 82.95 grams, with overall dimensions of 
approximately 3.5 by 5.7 centimetres and an internal annulus diameter close to 
2.2 centimetres.[2] These measurements place it among the larger known 
Mycenaean gold signet rings, which has implications for how it may have been 
worn and displayed as a marker of status.[2] The visible surface of the bezel 
carries a densely composed engraved scene, while the hoop is relatively thick 
to support the weight of the bezel.[2][3]\n\nAnalyses of Mycenaean signet rings
that incorporate the Ring of Nestor use precise measurements, X‑ray examination
and materials analysis to explore how such rings were manufactured and grouped 
by workshop. One related technical study by Nadine Engels (Becker) argues that 
a set of thirteen signet rings, including large gold examples from Crete and 
the Greek mainland, were produced by six different "masters" active during Late
Helladic II to Late Helladic III A, based on similarities in style, fabrication
and metalworking technique.[1] Commentators on the gold rings from the Griffin 
Warrior tomb at Pylos, which are often discussed alongside the Ring of Nestor, 
have emphasized that such rings were carved before the availability of 
microscopes and electric tools, highlighting the fine workmanship achieved by 
Bronze Age craftspeople working at very small scales.[3]\n\nThe engraving on 
the Ring of Nestor has been described in recent scholarship, including the 
study hosted on Academia.edu, as a complex composition that includes a female 
figure interpreted as a deity, possibly connected with Demeter, an eagle 
associated with Zeus, and additional elements such as grain and celestial 
motifs.[2] Some readings propose that four recurring features of the scene 
represent the four seasons, while a background that combines the sun and moon 
alludes to cyclical time and eternity in ritual thought.[2] These 
interpretations depend on detailed iconographic and mythological analysis and 
are constrained by limited direct evidence for the religious imagery of the 
Late Helladic II and Middle Helladic periods; the authors therefore stress the 
difficulty of reaching definitive identifications and treat the ring as a 
demanding object for the study of Bronze Age iconography.[2]', 'evidence_refs':
['e5', 'e6', 'e7', 'e8', 'e9', 'e10', 'e11', 'e12', 'e13', 'e18'], 
'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x1']}]})
